# One line ending in the working tree, on every machine.
#
# git stores LF and Windows checks out CRLF by default, which is invisible until
# something READS a source file as text - and the guard tests do exactly that:
# a manifest checked out as CRLF "declares no target" to AppsTests, and
# DocumentationTests splits on "\n" and reports every library file as
# undocumented at line 1.
#
# eol=lf rather than a per-extension list: the shell scripts need LF to run at
# all, the Swift, C#, MSBuild and JSON files are all read as text by something
# here, and a rule that names extensions is a rule somebody has to remember to
# extend.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The wire fixtures are bytes, not text, and text=auto only leaves alone what
# git DETECTS as binary - a NUL somewhere in the first 8000 bytes. A tiny
# fixture can be NUL-free, and one that then contained 0x0D 0x0A adjacent bytes
# would be silently rewritten at check-in, breaking the Swift-writes/C#-applies
# contract with nothing failing anywhere.
*.bin binary
